{"tag":"C0C9","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>Let <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"K = 2\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">K</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span>.</span> The consumer’s preferences are monotonic, continuous and strictly convex, and they are quasi-linear in the first commodity. As <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"w\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span></span></span></span></span> varies, what happens to the demand for commodity 1?</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>The characterization (our answer; the book asks how demand for the first commodity changes with <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"w\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span></span></span></span></span>):</span> below a wealth threshold <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"t\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span></span> none of commodity 1 is bought, and above it every additional unit of wealth is spent on commodity 1, at the rate <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"1/p₁\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1/</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> — unless commodity 1 is never bought at all.</p></div></div>"}